[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Composition vs Inheritance (Has-A vs Is-A) in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-Oriented Programming relies heavily on code reuse and relationship modeling. Two fundamental ways to achieve this are Inheritance (the "Is-A" relationship) and Composition (the "Has-A" relationship). Choosing the right approach is critical for creating maintainable, flexible, and scalable software architectures."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Inheritance: The Is-A Relationship"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance allows a new class (derived or child class) to adopt the properties and behaviors of an existing class (base or parent class). This represents an "Is-A" relationship. For example, a SportsCar "Is-A" Car."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class Vehicle {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle started.");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    public void drive() {
        System.out.println("Car is driving.");
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Promotes code reuse and enables polymorphism."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Creates tight coupling, makes code rigid, and can lead to the fragile base class problem where changes in the parent unexpectedly break child classes."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Composition: The Has-A Relationship"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Composition involves constructing classes out of references to other classes rather than inheriting from them. This represents a "Has-A" relationship. For example, a Car "Has-A" Engine and "Has-A" MusicSystem."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"class Engine {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Engine roaring.");
    }
}

class Car {
    private Engine engine;

    public Car() {
        this.engine = new Engine();
    }

    public void startCar() {
        engine.start();
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Loose coupling, highly flexible, easily testable via mocking, and adheres to the Principle of Least Privilege."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Requires writing more boilerplate forwarding methods if not managed carefully."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices: Favor Composition Over Inheritance"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"A golden rule in modern software engineering, as highlighted in design patterns literature, is to 'Favor composition over inheritance.' Use inheritance only when a true hierarchical Is-A relationship exists and polymorphism is strictly required across the entire family of types. For behavior sharing and modular design, rely on composition."}]
